EXERCISE A: USER MANUAL PROCEDURE

Topic: How to Create a GitHub Repository and Make Your First Commit

1. Introduction

As a Computer Science student, learning how to use GitHub is an important part of developing programming skills. GitHub makes it easier to store projects, keep track of changes, and share work with other developers.

In this guide, I will explain how a beginner can create a GitHub repository and make their first commit using the GitHub website.

I have kept the instructions simple so that even someone using GitHub for the first time can follow them without much difficulty.

2. Prerequisites

Before getting started, you will need:

A computer with a working internet connection.

A web browser such as Google Chrome or Microsoft Edge.

An active GitHub account.

Your GitHub username and password.

Basic computer skills, such as opening websites and clicking buttons.

3. Step-by-Step Procedure

Step 1: Open GitHub.

Open your preferred web browser and visit https://github.com.

Expected result: The GitHub homepage will appear on your screen.

Step 2: Sign in to your account.

Click the Sign in button and enter your GitHub username or email address and password.

Expected result: You will be taken to your GitHub dashboard.

Step 3: Open the repository creation page.

Click the plus (+) icon in the top-right corner and select New repository.

Expected result: A page containing the repository creation form will appear.

Step 4: Enter a repository name.

Type a name that describes your project. For example, you can use my-first-project.

Expected result: Your chosen name will appear in the repository name field.

Step 5: Choose repository visibility.

Select the Public option to make your repository accessible to other GitHub users.

Expected result: The Public option will be selected.

Step 6: Add a README file.

Select the checkbox labelled Add a README file.

Expected result: GitHub will prepare a README file for your repository.

Step 7: Create your repository.

Click the Create repository button.

Expected result: Your new repository will open, and you will see the README.md file.

Step 8: Open the README file for editing.

Click README.md and select the edit option represented by the pencil icon.

Expected result: The README file will open in an editing area where you can add information about your project.

Step 9: Add a project description.

Write a short description explaining what your project is about.

Expected result: Your project description will appear in the README editor.

Step 10: Commit your changes.

Click Commit changes, enter a short message describing your update, and confirm the commit.

Expected result: GitHub will save your changes and display the new commit in your repository history.

4. Screenshot Description

To make this guide easier to understand, I would include a screenshot of the completed GitHub repository.

The screenshot should show:

The name of the repository.

The README.md file.

The repository visibility showing Public.

The commit history confirming that changes have been saved.

This screenshot would help a beginner recognize a successfully created repository and understand what the final result should look like.

5. Troubleshooting

Common problem: Unable to create a repository because the name is unavailable.

A beginner may sometimes try to use a repository name that is already being used in their GitHub account.

How to solve it:

Choose another name that is not already being used. For example, if my-first-project is unavailable, try my-first-project-2026.

After changing the name, try creating the repository again.

6. Conclusion

Creating a GitHub repository is a simple but important skill for anyone learning programming.

Through this procedure, a beginner can learn how to create a repository, add project information, and save changes using commits.

Understanding these basic steps provides a good starting point for managing future programming projects and working with other developers.

EXERCISE B: API REFERENCE ENTRY

Topic: Creating a New Task in a Project Management Application

1. Overview

In a project management application, users need a simple way to create tasks and assign responsibilities to different team members.

This API endpoint is designed to make that possible. It allows an authenticated user to create a new task within an existing project.

When creating a task, the user can provide its title, description, assignee, due date, and priority level.

Once the task is successfully created, the system returns its details and assigns it a unique identification number.

2. HTTP Method and Endpoint

HTTP Method: POST

Endpoint Path:

/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/tasks

Example:

POST /api/v1/projects/101/tasks

The POST method is used because the purpose of this endpoint is to create a new resource on the server.

3. Description

This endpoint allows an authenticated user to add a new task to a particular project.

For example, a project manager may want to assign a database assignment to a team member and set a deadline for completing it.

The user must provide the task title, assignee ID, due date, and priority level. Providing a description is optional.

The system checks the submitted information before creating the task.

4. Request Parameters

The endpoint uses a path parameter and a JSON request body.

A. Path Parameters

Parameter

Data Type

Required

Description

projectId

Integer

Yes

Identifies the project where the new task will be added.

For example, in /api/v1/projects/101/tasks, the project ID is 101.

B. Query Parameters

This endpoint does not require query parameters because all the necessary task information is provided through the path and request body.

