The authentication token should be replaced with a valid access token belonging to the user.

6. Example Request Body (JSON)

Below is an example of how a user can send information to create a new task.

{
  "title": "Complete Database Assignment",
  "description": "Design and implement a school management database using MySQL.",
  "assigneeId": 25,
  "dueDate": "2026-12-05",
  "priority": "high"
}

Understanding the example:

title: Gives the task its name.

description: Explains what needs to be done.

assigneeId: Identifies user 25 as the person responsible for the task.

dueDate: Sets December 5, 2026, as the deadline.

priority: Marks the task as high priority.

This example shows how the different parameters work together when creating a task.

7. Successful Response

When the server receives valid information and successfully creates the task, it returns the HTTP status code:

201 Created

Below is an example of the response the user would receive.

{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Task created successfully.",
  "data": {
    "id": 501,
    "projectId": 101,
    "title": "Complete Database Assignment",
    "description": "Design and implement a school management database using MySQL.",
    "assigneeId": 25,
    "dueDate": "2026-12-05",
    "priority": "high",
    "status": "pending",
    "createdAt": "2026-11-21T10:30:00Z"
  }
}

Explanation:

The response confirms that the task has been created successfully.

The system automatically generates a unique task ID of 501. It also confirms the project, assigned user, deadline, and priority level.

The initial task status is set to pending, meaning the task has been created but has not yet been completed.

